<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Aversa</h1><p class="st-meta">1030 CE – 1058 CE · capital Aversa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Norman lordship in southern Italy, granted to mercenaries in 1030 and the springboard for the Norman conquest of the south.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1030 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-iv-of-naples">Sergius IV</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a> rewarded the Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainulf-drengot">Rainulf Drengot</a> with the town and district of Aversa, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a>, to guard his frontier against the Principality of Capua. Rainulf's band became a magnet for further adventurers arriving from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Normandy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Aversa was a military lordship in which a small warrior elite of French-speaking Normans ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> and Italo-Romance population. Its counts sold their swords to whichever of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Salerno</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> paid best, gaining recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Conrad II</a> in 1038. A bishopric was established in the town in the mid-11th century, and the county drew in Norman knights who later served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1058 Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-capua">Richard Drengot</a> captured <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a> and took the title of prince. Aversa was absorbed into the Principality of Capua, and its warriors had by then helped launch the conquests that created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1030 CE – 1058 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.20%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1030 CE – 1058 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aversa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rainulf Drengot, Rainulf II Trincanocte, Richard I of Capua</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norman French, Italo-Romance</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aversa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1044&amp;focus=county-of-aversa" aria-label="County of Aversa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5028 715 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Aversa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5348 971L5349 976M5348 971L5362 981M5348 971L5349 965"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5362 981h0M5349 965h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5348 971h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5348 971h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Aversa. <a href="/atlas?year=1044&amp;focus=county-of-aversa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
